---
import GuideLayout from '../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
    {
        question: 'What is PR content repurposing?',
        answer:
            'It is the practice of taking one piece of public relations content — usually a press release or announcement — and producing several publishable assets from it: a blog article, a short brief, a social thread, an email snippet, and a newsletter item.',
    },
    {
        question: 'How many assets can I get from a single press release?',
        answer:
            'With PressFolio you can produce a full news article, a quick brief, and a tweet thread from the same input without retyping anything. Each reuses the same extracted source, quotes, and figures.',
    },
    {
        question: 'Why repurpose instead of writing each format separately?',
        answer:
            'Repurposing keeps the facts consistent. When every format derives from the same parsed source, the figures and quotes cannot drift between the blog post and the social thread.',
    },
];
---

<GuideLayout
    eyebrow="Workflow"
    heading="PR Content Repurposing From a Single Press Release"
    intro="Most PR teams write one strong release and then run out of time to publish it everywhere. This guide shows how to turn a single press release into a blog article, a short brief, and a social thread while keeping every figure and quote consistent."
    description="Turn one press release into a blog article, a short brief, a social thread, and newsletter copy while keeping every figure and quote consistent."
    breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'PR Content Repurposing', path: '/pr-content-repurposing' }]}
    faqs={faqs}
>
    <h2>The repurposing problem</h2>
    <p>
        A press release is written for journalists, not for readers. It front-loads the dateline, buries the
        interesting detail under official phrasing, and assumes the reader already cares. That is why repurposing is
        manual work: every destination format needs a different opening, a different length, and a different level of
        formality.
    </p>
    <p>
        The trap is that each format is rewritten from scratch, and each rewrite is a chance for a number to change.
        Repurposing from one parsed source removes that risk. Parse the release once, then generate each output from
        the same extracted facts.
    </p>

    <h2>The asset stack from one release</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Asset</th><th>Source in the release</th><th>Channel</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Blog article</td><td>Headline, context, quote, figures</td><td>Company blog, newsroom</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Quick brief</td><td>Headline, dateline, strongest figure</td><td>Newsletter, internal update</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tweet thread</td><td>Headline, one fact per post, quote</td><td>Social</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email snippet</td><td>Opening paragraph</td><td>Outreach, investor updates</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Meta description</td><td>Headline plus primary figure</td><td>Search and social previews</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>A practical sequence</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Parse once.</strong> Paste the release and let the converter extract source, date, location, headline, quote, figures, and context.</li>
        <li><strong>Correct the facts first.</strong> Fix names, numbers, and dates in the refine stage before generating anything, so every output inherits the corrected data.</li>
        <li><strong>Generate the long form.</strong> Produce the article first — it needs the most context and will expose gaps in the source material.</li>
        <li><strong>Derive the short forms.</strong> Produce the brief and thread from the same fields. Nothing needs retyping.</li>
        <li><strong>Publish in parallel.</strong> Export Markdown for the CMS, HTML for a newsletter tool, and the thread for social.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Keeping consistency across formats</h2>
    <p>
        Two rules keep repurposed content honest. First, never introduce a claim in a derived format that is not in
        the parsed source — if the thread needs a stat the release does not contain, that is a gap in the release, not
        a writing problem. Second, keep the attribution identical everywhere: the same speaker, the same title, the
        same organisation, in the article, the brief, and the thread.
    </p>

    <h2>Where PressFolio fits</h2>
    <p>
        PressFolio handles the extraction and the formatting for the article, brief, and thread formats from a single
        input. It also gives you a clean Markdown export, which is the easiest format to hand to a CMS or a
        newsletter tool. See <a href="/press-release-to-blog-post">how the converter works</a>, or read the
        <a href="/blog/press-release-seo-checklist">press release SEO checklist</a> before you publish.
    </p>
</GuideLayout>